PART 2: UNDERSTANDING STATE IN REACT
================================================================================

WHAT IS STATE?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

State in React is:

• Internal Component Data
  - Information managed by a component

• Mutable Data
  - Can be changed and updated over time through its state setter

• Triggers Re-renders
  - When state changes, React schedules the component to render again

• Component-Specific
  - Each component instance can maintain its own state

• Managed with Hooks
  - In functional components, state is commonly managed using useState

• Reactive
  - State updates are reflected in the user interface

• Persistent During Component Lifecycle
  - State is preserved across renders while that component instance
    remains mounted


STATE VS PROPS: KEY DIFFERENCES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

+-------------------+---------------------------+----------------------------+
| CHARACTERISTIC    | STATE                     | PROPS                      |
+-------------------+---------------------------+----------------------------+
| Source            | Internal to component     | Passed from parent         |
| Mutability        | Updated using setter      | Read-only                  |
| Re-render Trigger | Yes, when changed         | Yes, when changed          |
| Management Method | useState hook             | Function parameters        |
| Ownership         | Component owns it         | Parent owns the data       |
| Use Case          | Dynamic/changing data     | Data/configuration         |
| Scope             | Local to component        | Passed down component tree |
| Modification      | Via setter function       | Cannot modify in child     |
+-------------------+---------------------------+----------------------------+


WHY STATE MATTERS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

State is critical because:

• Enables Interactivity
  - Components can respond to user actions

• Manages Data Changes
  - Tracks how data evolves over time

• Drives UI Updates
  - State changes update what users see

• Enables Form Handling
  - Forms use state to track input values

• Manages Component Behavior
  - Controls what a component displays and does

• Maintains Data During Component Lifecycle
  - State remains available across renders while the component is mounted


================================================================================
PART 3: THE useState HOOK - COMPLETE GUIDE
================================================================================

UNDERSTANDING useState SYNTAX
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The useState hook follows this pattern:

const [state, setState] = useState(initialValue)


BREAKDOWN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

+---------------------+------------------------------------------------+
| PART                | MEANING                                        |
+---------------------+------------------------------------------------+
| const               | Declares variables                             |
| state               | Current state value                            |
| setState            | Function used to update the state              |
| useState()          | React hook that creates/manages state           |
| initialValue        | Value used during initial state creation        |
| Return Value        | [currentValue, updateFunction]                  |
+---------------------+------------------------------------------------+


HOW useState WORKS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Initialization
   - When the component is initialized, useState uses the initial value

        ↓

2. Storage
   - React associates the state value with that component instance

        ↓

3. Return
   - Returns the current value and an update function

        ↓

4. Update
   - Calling the setter requests a state update

        ↓

5. Re-render
   - React renders the component with the updated state

        ↓

6. Persistence
   - State persists across renders while the component remains mounted


